  "query_preview": "(This evening through Saturday)...\nIssued at 200 PM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nSurface analysis this afternoon shows a large area of high pressure \nin place through the Mississippi Valley. Cool northwest flow was in \nplace across Indiana. GOES19 shows some lake cloud steaming from \nLake MI, pushing south across north central Indiana. Aloft, a highly \namplified pattern was in place. A sharp omega block was in place \nwith ridging over the plains states, low pressure over the northeast \nand a deep trough in place over the Pacific northwest extending into \nthe intermountain west. Dew points across the area were in the lower \n30s.\n\nTonight...\n\nThe strong ridging aloft to the west will nudge slightly eastward. \nIndiana will continue to remain under the influence of lee side \nsubsidence and NW flow aloft through the overnight hours. Meanwhile \nat the surface, the strong high pressure system will also nudge \neast, arriving over IL and WI. This will keep light NW flow across \nIndiana, but should result in clearing skies across our area as the \nlake clouds drifts farther to the northeast and heating is lost. Dew \npoint depressions overnight may fall to less than 2F. This with \nclearing skies and light winds will be a good set-up for radiational \ncooling and fog. Look for overnight lows around 30.\n\nSunday and Monday...\n\nThe strong ridging aloft will build east across Indiana on Sunday \nand Monday. Furthermore, a secondary ridge aloft is suggested to \ndevelop within the pattern over the Central Plains on Monday, \nreinforcing subsidence across the area. Meanwhile within the lower \nlevels, the strong surface high pressure system to west will build \neastward, settling over Indiana and Ohio on Monday. This will bring \nmore dry and cool weather to our area for this time. Forecast \nsoundings on Sunday suggest that convective temperatures may be \nreached producing some flat topped stratocu, but more sunshine is \nindicated on Monday with a dry column in play. Thus a partly cloudy \nSunday and mostly sunny Monday will be in store. Slight warm air \nadvection is indicated for Sunday and Monday. Thus highs around 50 \non Sunday should warm to the lower 50s for Monday. These values are \nat or below seasonal normals.\n\nTuesday through Saturday...\n\nSurface high pressure positioned over the central CONUS will slowly \nsettle into the region through the week. Deep subsidence and \nincreasing heights from the subsequent surface high will keep skies \nmostly clear through late Wednesday. During the day Wednesday, a \nsubtle shortwave trough will start to take shape over the Great \nPlains. A subsequent surface low pressure system is expected to form \nover the Nebraska/South Dakota vicinity with a cold front extending \nsouth towards the MO River Valley. While the subsequent surface low \nwill remain well north of central Indiana, subtle isentropic ascent \nwill promote the development of SCT to BKN mid to high level clouds \nthrough the rest of the forecast period. While rain chances look to \nhold off until Sunday morning at the earliest, models indicate a 10-\n20% chance an isolated shower or two occurring Thursday through \nSaturday morning. However, confidence is too low at this time to \ninclude any mention in the forecast.\n\n10-20 kts of southerly WAA from the subsequent surface high pressure \nwill usher in the transition from more seasonable high temperatures \nin the low to mid 50s Tuesday with low's in the mid 30s to \ntemperatures 10-15 degrees above average by the end of the week. \nHigh's by then will approach the mid to upper 60s with lows in the \nupper 40s.",
